This compilation error is happening because your BatchProcessingException class currently only has one constructor that requires both a Message and a Throwable cause. Since we are throwing a new error based on a timeout or a generic failure, we need to add a single-argument constructor.
Here are the two updated files to fix this error immediately.
1. Updated: CustomExceptions.java
Add the single-argument constructor to BatchProcessingException so it matches the pattern we used for DatabaseOperationException.
package com.fincore.JournalService.Exception;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ResponseStatus;

public class CustomExceptions {

    // ... (keep other exceptions as they are)

    @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.INTERNAL_SERVER_ERROR)
    public static class BatchProcessingException extends RuntimeException {
        // Rule 1: Added single-argument constructor to fix the "Expected 2 arguments" error
        public BatchProcessingException(String message) { 
            super(message); 
        }

        public BatchProcessingException(String message, Throwable cause) { 
            super(message, cause); 
        }
    }
    
    // ...
}

2. Updated Catch Block in JournalRequestServiceImpl.java
Now that the constructor is added, your code will compile. I have also refined the ExecutionException catch to properly pass the "cause" to the exception, which satisfies the 2-argument requirement for that specific case.
/**
 * Rule 1: Specific Exception Handling.
 * Fixed the argument count error by providing the cause where available 
 * and using the new single-argument constructor for timeouts.
 */
try {
    CompletableFuture.allOf(futures.toArray(new CompletableFuture[0]))
            .get(30, TimeUnit.MINUTES);
} catch (java.util.concurrent.TimeoutException te) {
    log.error("BATCH_TIMEOUT | Batch: {} | Status: Processing exceeded 30 minutes", batchId);
    // Now works with 1 argument
    throw new CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException("Batch creation timed out. Check Oracle performance.");
} catch (InterruptedException | java.util.concurrent.ExecutionException e) {
    log.error("ASYNC_EXECUTION_ERROR | Batch: {} | Type: {}", batchId, e.getClass().getSimpleName());
    // Uses 2 arguments (Message + Cause) to preserve the original error stack
    throw new CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException("Parallel execution failed.", e.getCause());
} catch (CompletionException e) {
    log.error("ASYNC_COMPLETION_ERROR | Batch: {} | Status: Thread join failure", batchId);
    throw new CustomExceptions.BatchProcessingException("Background task interrupted.", e.getCause());
}

Why this fixes it:
 * TimeoutException: This is a logical timeout, not a code crash, so there is no "cause" (Throwable) to pass. The new 1-argument constructor handles this.
 * ExecutionException: This happens when a worker thread actually crashes. By using e.getCause(), we pass the real error (like a SqlException) into our custom exception, which helps with debugging.
